<https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/claim/morphoeval-bavestrello-sponge-spicule-hexactin> a pk1:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Uriz et al. (2003) & Aizenberg et al. (2005) sponge spicule silicatein axial symmetry, concentric biosilica lamination, Euplectella checkerboard lattice mechanics, cohesive-zone laminate bending, helical cage ridges and basalia waveguiding (Euplectella aspergillum (Hexactinellida) & Tethya aurantia / Pachastrella monilifera (Demospongiae) / Sycon raphanus (Calcarea))",
        "Uriz et al. (2003) & Aizenberg et al. (2005) sponge spicule silicatein axial symmetry, concentric biosilica lamination, Euplectella checkerboard lattice mechanics, cohesive-zone laminate bending, helical cage ridges and basalia waveguiding faithfully reproduces Euplectella aspergillum (Hexactinellida) & Tethya aurantia / Pachastrella monilifera (Demospongiae) / Sycon raphanus (Calcarea) morphology" ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/bavestrello-sponge-spicule-hexactin-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/bavestrello-sponge-spicule-hexactin-2>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/bavestrello-sponge-spicule-hexactin-3>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/bavestrello-sponge-spicule-hexactin-4>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/bavestrello-sponge-spicule-hexactin-5>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/bavestrello-sponge-spicule-hexactin-6>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/bavestrello-sponge-spicule-hexactin-7> ;
    dct:description "The simulation script(s) models/2012-bavestrello-sponge-spicule-hexactin.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Euplectella aspergillum (Hexactinellida) & Tethya aurantia / Pachastrella monilifera (Demospongiae) / Sycon raphanus (Calcarea) specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is the weight of evidence (MorphoEval 0.1.0 overall 88.6/100, audit 2026-10-19-sponge-spicule-hexactin-audit-v3) and the assessments." ;
    dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/a69596cc/models/2012-bavestrello-sponge-spicule-hexactin.py> ;
    schema:about "Euplectella aspergillum (Hexactinellida) & Tethya aurantia / Pachastrella monilifera (Demospongiae) / Sycon raphanus (Calcarea)" ;
    schema:additionalProperty "does_not_claim: A 3D cohesive-zone finite-element reproduction of Weaver et al. (2007) load-displacement curves, measured ridge geometry from the specimen, measured numerical aperture or mode images of real basalia, or independent verification of any Pass 3 DOI or recalled constant",
        "reconstructed_in_script: O_h / T_d / D_3h / D_inf_h silicatein ray vectors, a 3D silicic-acid reaction-diffusion accretion solver with k_cat ablation, the h_n = h_1 r_1 / r_n lamellar law, checkerboard diagonal shear stiffness, a 12-species symmetry/toughness table, DoG + gradient-tangent segmentation of the vendored Euplectella photograph; Pass 3: a 48-element Hermite-beam FE three-point-bend of the laminate with a bilinear cohesive interlayer, He-Hutchinson deflection criterion and Griffith delamination, coupled to the 3D solver through the accreted cross-section; helical-ridge torsion / ovalization gain; step-index basalia waveguide with meridional ray tracing and LP-mode counting",
        "reported_in_source: Aizenberg et al. (2005): Euplectella spicules are concentric silica lamellae (thickness ~1.5 um at the centre to ~0.2 um at the periphery) separated by organic interlayers; the cylindrical cage carries alternating diagonal bracing and external helical/oblique ridges; basalia act as optical fibres",
        "reported_in_source: Sundar et al. (2003) / Aizenberg et al. (2004): basalia have a high-index core and lower-index lamellar cladding and guide light (values recalled offline)",
        "reported_in_source: Uriz et al. (2003): silicatein axial filaments with crystallographic symmetry template the ray geometry of siliceous spicules (monaxon, triactine, tetractin, hexactin)",
        "reported_in_source: Weaver et al. (2007): laminated spicules fail gracefully under three-point bending through crack deflection at the organic interlayers, unlike monolithic silica rods",
        "transfer_boundary: Ridge count, pitch and area ratio, the cohesive constants (sigma_f, G_s, T_max, delta_c), span/diameter, and the optical-core lamella count are modelling choices tagged `provenance: model`; E = 36 GPa, the He-Hutchinson 0.25 ratio, n_core / n_clad and the Aizenberg lamella thicknesses are `recalled` and unverified",
        "transfer_boundary: The 12-species theta_obs_deg / toughness_ratio_obs columns are Pass 1 recalled numbers, not digitised measurements; their r = 1.0000 agreement with the model laws is not independent validation",
        "transfer_boundary: The FE bending model is 1D (single midspan crack, outermost-in strength criterion, no friction, bridging or shear-lag) on the Law-3 geometry (outer radius 5.83 um, span 233 um), not Weaver et al.'s 3D cohesive-zone specimen geometry (r = 22.5 um in the species table); it predicts W_lam / W_mono = 1.71, not the 8.95 of the table nor the 9.0 of the Pass 2 eta-law" ;
    pk1:aristotelianEfficientCause "8. Crystal Accretion & TPMS" ;
    pk1:aristotelianMegistonGenos "II. Anaima (Invertebrata)" ;
    pk1:aristotelianPoionForm "Coiled Shells, Spicules & Gyroids" ;
    pk1:aristotelianPosonLaw "Raup (W,D,S) / Gyroid Mean H=0" ;
    pk1:aristotelianSubalternClass "Zoophyta & Vermes (Cnidaria/Platyhelminthes)" ;
    pk1:biologicalGenus "Euplectella" ;
    pk1:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/a69596cc/models/2012-bavestrello-sponge-spicule-hexactin.py; output: docs/media/sponge_spicule_hexactin_comparison.png; organism: Euplectella aspergillum (Hexactinellida) & Tethya aurantia / Pachastrella monilifera (Demospongiae) / Sycon raphanus (Calcarea); checkpoint commit: https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/commit/a69596cc; environment: python 3.14.7, numpy 2.4.2, scipy 1.17.0, matplotlib 3.10.8, Pillow 12.1.1; spec_fingerprint: e8c71f657d97f3086b3a9576cab7f81ff7eec29a8903ce345cec0985c35812ca; artifact_sha256: 861ad8a2014aa03f8fa9f126adcae9bf5150b78de2e1099f2b37099dc5e14772; input_dataset_sha256: {datasets/sponge_spicule_hexactin/cohesive_bending_waveguide_reference.json=433f783ec40a0abc..., datasets/sponge_spicule_hexactin/euplectella_aspergillum_specimen.jpg=b56275f43e5ea078..., datasets/sponge_spicule_hexactin/scout_manifest.json=fb6e06653d9d14ac..., datasets/sponge_spicule_hexactin/sponge_spicule_hexactin_reference.json=b9dd33b60b05730c...}; supersedes_audit_id: 2026-10-19-sponge-spicule-hexactin-audit-v2; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Uriz et al. (2003): silicatein axial filaments with crystallographic symmetry template the ray geometry of siliceous spicules (monaxon, triactine, tetractin, hexactin) | Aizenberg et al. (2005): Euplectella spicules are concentric silica lamellae (thickness ~1.5 um at the centre to ~0.2 um at the periphery) separated by organic interlayers; the cylindrical cage carries alternating diagonal bracing and external helical/oblique ridges; basalia act as optical fibres | Weaver et al. (2007): laminated spicules fail gracefully under three-point bending through crack deflection at the organic interlayers, unlike monolithic silica rods | Sundar et al. (2003) / Aizenberg et al. (2004): basalia have a high-index core and lower-index lamellar cladding and guide light (values recalled offline); reconstructed_in_script: O_h / T_d / D_3h / D_inf_h silicatein ray vectors, a 3D silicic-acid reaction-diffusion accretion solver with k_cat ablation, the h_n = h_1 r_1 / r_n lamellar law, checkerboard diagonal shear stiffness, a 12-species symmetry/toughness table, DoG + gradient-tangent segmentation of the vendored Euplectella photograph; Pass 3: a 48-element Hermite-beam FE three-point-bend of the laminate with a bilinear cohesive interlayer, He-Hutchinson deflection criterion and Griffith delamination, coupled to the 3D solver through the accreted cross-section; helical-ridge torsion / ovalization gain; step-index basalia waveguide with meridional ray tracing and LP-mode counting; transfer_boundary: The FE bending model is 1D (single midspan crack, outermost-in strength criterion, no friction, bridging or shear-lag) on the Law-3 geometry (outer radius 5.83 um, span 233 um), not Weaver et al.'s 3D cohesive-zone specimen geometry (r = 22.5 um in the species table); it predicts W_lam / W_mono = 1.71, not the 8.95 of the table nor the 9.0 of the Pass 2 eta-law | Ridge count, pitch and area ratio, the cohesive constants (sigma_f, G_s, T_max, delta_c), span/diameter, and the optical-core lamella count are modelling choices tagged `provenance: model`; E = 36 GPa, the He-Hutchinson 0.25 ratio, n_core / n_clad and the Aizenberg lamella thicknesses are `recalled` and unverified | The 12-species theta_obs_deg / toughness_ratio_obs columns are Pass 1 recalled numbers, not digitised measurements; their r = 1.0000 agreement with the model laws is not independent validation; does_not_claim: A 3D cohesive-zone finite-element reproduction of Weaver et al. (2007) load-displacement curves, measured ridge geometry from the specimen, measured numerical aperture or mode images of real basalia, or independent verification of any Pass 3 DOI or recalled constant]" ;
    pk1:derivedFromPaper <https://doi.org/10.1002/jemt.10395>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1016/0008-8846(76)90007-7>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-7683(89)90021-8>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsb.2007.01.006>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1038/424899a>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0307843101>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.95.11.6234>,
