    rdfs:label "Uriz et al. (2003) & Aizenberg et al. (2005) sponge spicule silicatein axial symmetry, concentric biosilica lamination, Euplectella checkerboard lattice mechanics, cohesive-zone laminate bending, helical cage ridges and basalia waveguiding — Morphological realism / specimen match: 88/100" ;
    dct:date "2026-10-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:description "The Pass 2 DoG / gradient-tangent segmentation of the SHA-256-locked *Euplectella aspergillum* photograph (L592-667; orthogonal 89.65 deg, diagonal 47.68 deg, pitch 2.50 mm) is retained and the main 6-panel figure (v3 title) still shows the specimen overlay in panel E and now plots the FE cohesive-zone prediction as a star against the eta-law and the 12-species points in panel D (L1408-1422). The new Pass 3 figure (6 panels: P-delta traces, G_i sweep, k_cat -> N coupling, ridge torsion vs psi, ovalization gain, waveguide mode map) and the 10-frame bending-fracture GIF render the new mechanics. Deductions: nothing new is measured from the specimen — ridge count, pitch and area are `provenance: model` and the photograph is not used to count ridges or estimate the helix angle (-4); the laminate geometry the bending / optics models use (outer radius 5.83 um) is one-quarter of the 22.5 um the species table gives for Euplectella (-3); the specimen reference angles 89.6498 / 47.6843 stored in the JSON are the script's own outputs, so the \"match\" is a regression check (-3); the main PNG panel titles A/B/C overlap horizontally at fontsize ~9.5 and the Pass 3 panel F plots modes vs 4 lambda on the radius axis (-2)." ;
